Verdict: PASS. The diff does what #1010 asked; I wrote the full verdict to session_plan/eval_task_1.md. I didn't re-run the suite (the harness already passed it) or the binary, so this rests on reading the diff, a few greps and one issue lookup.

• **Original check unchanged:** user_deny_refusal still runs the old whole-command match first, with the same refusal wording.
• **Each part of a compound is now checked:** if the whole command doesn't match, it's split into parts at `;`, `&&`, `|` and so on, but never inside quotes. Leading `VAR=value`, `export`-style keywords and words like if/then are stripped, and each part is matched against the deny patterns.
• **Indirect program names are refused:** if no part matches and a deny list exists, a command whose program name comes from `$X`, `$(...)` or backticks is refused. The two refusal messages are worded differently and pass the model's text through sanitize_for_display.
• **No deny list, no change:** an empty deny list returns early, so users without one see the same behaviour as before. This repo's `.yoyo.toml` sets no deny list, so the project's own runs aren't affected.
• **Tests:** they cover every must-refuse and must-allow case the task listed, plus a heredoc case. They also pin `"$CARGO" build` behaving differently with and without a deny list, and the eval "$(...)" gap as a stated limit.
• **Every caller goes through the one function:** both bash tools (main agent at `tools.rs:377`, sub-agent at `:1649`) reach `tools::user_deny_refusal` via `cd_deny::effective_user_deny_refusal`. Deny patterns added by /cd count as a deny list, so the $VAR refusal applies to them too, which is consistent.
• **No unused definitions:** the new function is re-exported and called, and the new keyword list and splitter are both used.
• Docs: `permissions.md item 1 is rewritten to match the new behaviour, including the eval` limit.
• **Follow-up issue:** the eval "$(...)" gap is filed as #1011, open with the agent-self label.

